// Owner question set 2 (V1.15), part A: modern Java (11-21), Java 8 features, Stream API.
// Questions supplied by the project owner; answers, key points and follow-ups written for V1.15.
import { kp, Q } from './kp.js';

const MJ = 'Modern Java (11-21)', J8 = 'Java 8+ Features';
const src = { source: 'owner-set2' };

export const V115_A = [
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1. Java versions and modern features
  Q('nq-001', MJ, 'EMS', 'concept', 'Which Java/JDK version are you currently using in your projects?',
    [kp('Name the version and that it is an LTS release', 'lts', 'long term support', 'java 17', 'java 21', 'java 11'), kp('Why that version: support, Spring Boot 3 baseline', 'spring boot 3', 'support', 'baseline'), kp('Features you actually use from it', 'record*', 'virtual thread*', 'switch', 'text block*'), kp('Upgrade path / migration experience', 'upgrad*', 'migrat*')],
    'Answer with a concrete version and why. For example: we run Java 21, the current long term support release, and we moved from Java 11 to 17 and then 21. Spring Boot 3 needs at least Java 17, and staying on an LTS gives us security patches for years. From these versions we use records for DTOs, switch expressions and pattern matching, text blocks for SQL and JSON in tests, and we are trying virtual threads for blocking I/O services. Mention what the upgrade involved: updating build plugins, removed APIs such as javax to jakarta, and running the test suite on the new JDK before switching production images.',
    'What problems did you hit when upgrading the JDK?', src),
  Q('nq-002', MJ, 'MS', 'concept', 'What major features were introduced after Java 8, particularly in Java 11, Java 17, and Java 21?',
    [kp('Java 11: var in lambdas, HTTP client, String methods, single-file run', 'http client', 'httpclient', 'isblank', 'strip', 'lines', 'var'), kp('Java 17: records, sealed classes, pattern matching instanceof, text blocks, switch expressions', 'record*', 'sealed', 'pattern matching', 'text block*', 'switch expression*'), kp('Java 21: virtual threads, record patterns, pattern matching for switch, sequenced collections', 'virtual thread*', 'record pattern*', 'sequenced'), kp('Six-month release cadence with LTS every two years', 'lts', 'six month*', 'release cadence')],
    'Since Java 9 there is a release every six months, with long term support releases 11, 17 and 21. Java 9 to 11 brought modules, var for local variables, collection factories like List.of, the standard HttpClient, new String methods such as isBlank, strip, lines and repeat, and running a single source file with java File.java. Java 17 made records, sealed classes, text blocks, switch expressions and pattern matching for instanceof standard, plus helpful NullPointerException messages. Java 21 added virtual threads, pattern matching for switch, record patterns, sequenced collections, and previews of structured concurrency and scoped values. Garbage collectors also improved a lot, with ZGC and G1 enhancements.',
    'Which of these features has had the most impact on your code?', src),
  Q('nq-003', MJ, 'MS', 'concept', 'What Java 17 and Java 21 features have you used or explored?',
    [kp('Records for DTOs and value objects', 'record*', 'dto'), kp('Switch expressions and pattern matching', 'switch', 'pattern matching', 'instanceof'), kp('Text blocks', 'text block*'), kp('Virtual threads for blocking I/O', 'virtual thread*'), kp('Sealed classes / sequenced collections with a concrete use', 'sealed', 'sequenced', 'getfirst', 'example')],
    'Give concrete uses. With Java 17 I use records for request and response DTOs and event payloads, which removes getters, equals and hashCode boilerplate. Switch expressions with arrows return a value and need no break, and pattern matching for instanceof removes casts. Text blocks keep SQL and JSON readable in tests. Sealed interfaces model a closed set of results, such as Success and Failure. With Java 21 I enabled virtual threads in Spring Boot with spring.threads.virtual.enabled so blocking HTTP and database calls scale without a big thread pool, used pattern matching for switch over sealed types, and sequenced collections methods like getFirst and reversed.',
    'Where would you avoid virtual threads?', src),
  Q('nq-004', MJ, 'EM', 'concept', 'What are records in Java, and how do you define one?',
    [kp('Concise class for immutable data carriers', 'immutable', 'data carrier*', 'data class'), kp('Declared with record Name(components)', 'record', 'component*'), kp('Compiler generates constructor, accessors, equals, hashCode, toString', 'constructor', 'accessor*', 'equals', 'hashcode', 'tostring'), kp('Fields are private final; class is final', 'final', 'private')],
    'A record, standard since Java 16, is a special class for transparent, immutable data carriers. You declare it as record Point(int x, int y) {}. From the header the compiler generates private final fields, a canonical constructor, accessor methods named x() and y(), and equals, hashCode and toString based on all components. The record class is implicitly final and extends java.lang.Record. You can add a compact constructor for validation, static factory methods, extra instance methods and implement interfaces. Records are ideal for DTOs, API responses, map keys, and values returned from streams.',
    'How do you validate fields in a record?', src),
  Q('nq-005', MJ, 'EM', 'concept', 'What is the difference between a record and a regular Java class?',
    [kp('Record fields are final; no setters', 'final', 'setter*', 'immutable'), kp('Record cannot extend another class (implicitly extends Record), is final', 'extend*', 'inherit*', 'final'), kp('Boilerplate generated automatically', 'generat*', 'boilerplate', 'equals', 'hashcode'), kp('No extra instance fields beyond components', 'instance field*', 'components'), kp('Regular class for mutable state or behaviour, e.g. JPA entities', 'entity', 'entities', 'jpa', 'mutable')],
    'A regular class gives full control: mutable fields, setters, inheritance, any constructor. A record is a restricted, final class whose state is exactly its components: fields are private and final, it cannot extend another class because it already extends java.lang.Record, and you cannot declare extra instance fields. In return the compiler generates the constructor, accessors, equals, hashCode and toString. Records can still implement interfaces, have static fields and methods, and instance methods. Use records for value data; use regular classes for objects with mutable state or identity, such as JPA entities, which need a no-argument constructor and setters.',
    'Why are records a poor fit for JPA entities?', src),
  Q('nq-006', MJ, 'EM', 'concept', 'Are records immutable, and can you define accessor, getter, or setter methods in them?',
    [kp('Shallowly immutable: final fields, but referenced objects can change', 'shallow*', 'final', 'mutable list', 'defensive cop*'), kp('Accessors are generated, named after the component; can be overridden', 'accessor*', 'override', 'name()'), kp('No setters possible because fields are final', 'setter*', 'cannot'), kp('Use copies like List.copyOf in the compact constructor', 'list.copyof', 'copyof', 'compact constructor')],
    'Records are shallowly immutable. Their fields are final, so after construction you cannot reassign them, and you cannot write a setter that changes a field. But if a component is a mutable object such as an ArrayList, the list itself can still be modified, so for real immutability make a defensive copy in the compact constructor, for example items = List.copyOf(items). The compiler generates accessor methods named after components, like name(), not getName(). You may override an accessor explicitly, for example to return an unmodifiable view, and add other methods such as a getName style method or withName that returns a new record.',
    'How would you implement a "wither" method on a record?', src),
  Q('nq-007', MJ, 'MS', 'concept', 'What are sealed classes, and how do they restrict inheritance?',
    [kp('sealed modifier with permits clause lists allowed subclasses', 'sealed', 'permits'), kp('Subclasses must be final, sealed or non-sealed', 'final', 'non-sealed'), kp('Closed hierarchy for domain modelling', 'closed', 'domain', 'hierarch*'), kp('Exhaustive switch / pattern matching without default', 'exhaustive', 'switch', 'pattern matching')],
    'A sealed class or interface, standard since Java 17, declares exactly which classes may extend it: public sealed class Shape permits Circle, Square, Rectangle. Every permitted subclass must be final, sealed again, or non-sealed to reopen the hierarchy, and they must be in the same module or package. This gives a closed hierarchy, a middle ground between final and fully open inheritance. It documents the design, and the compiler can check exhaustiveness: a switch over a sealed type that covers all permitted subtypes needs no default branch, and adding a new subtype makes incomplete switches fail to compile.',
    'Why is exhaustive switching useful when the domain changes?', src),
  Q('nq-008', MJ, 'MS', 'concept', 'Can sealed classes be applied to interfaces?',
    [kp('Yes: sealed interface with permits', 'sealed interface', 'yes', 'permits'), kp('Implementations can be records, which are final', 'record*'), kp('Used to model algebraic data types like Result', 'result', 'success', 'failure', 'algebraic'), kp('Works with pattern matching switch', 'switch', 'pattern')],
    'Yes. An interface can be sealed in the same way: sealed interface PaymentResult permits Approved, Declined, Pending. The permitted implementations can be classes or records, and records fit well because they are implicitly final. This is the common way to model a closed set of alternatives, like a Result type with Success and Failure, or commands and events in a domain. Combined with Java 21 pattern matching for switch, you can write switch (result) { case Approved a -> ...; case Declined d -> ...; case Pending p -> ...; } and the compiler verifies every case is handled.',
    'How does a sealed interface compare with an enum?', src),
  Q('nq-009', MJ, 'EM', 'concept', 'What switch-related enhancements were introduced in newer Java versions?',
    [kp('Switch expressions return a value (Java 14)', 'switch expression*', 'return* a value', 'yield'), kp('Arrow labels, no fall-through, multiple labels', 'arrow', 'fall through', 'fall-through', 'break'), kp('Pattern matching for switch with types (Java 21)', 'pattern matching', 'type pattern*', 'case integer'), kp('Guards with when, null handling, exhaustiveness', 'when', 'guard*', 'null', 'exhaustive')],
    'Java 14 made switch an expression: int days = switch (month) { case FEB -> 28; case APR, JUN, SEP, NOV -> 30; default -> 31; }. Arrow labels do not fall through, so no break is needed, several labels can share a case, and a block uses yield to return a value. Java 21 added pattern matching for switch: you can switch on any object and match types, case String s -> ..., case Integer i when i > 10 -> ..., with guards using when, a case null branch, and record deconstruction patterns. Over enums and sealed types the compiler checks exhaustiveness, which removes a whole class of missing-case bugs.',
    'What does yield do in a switch expression?', src),
  Q('nq-010', MJ, 'E', 'concept', 'How do text blocks simplify multiline strings?',
    [kp('Triple quotes delimit a multiline string', 'triple quote*', 'three quote*', 'triple double'), kp('No escaping of quotes and newlines', 'escap*', 'newline*', '\\n'), kp('Incidental indentation removed automatically', 'indent*'), kp('Use for SQL, JSON, HTML in code and tests; formatted() for values', 'sql', 'json', 'html', 'formatted')],
    'Text blocks, standard since Java 15, are string literals between triple double quotes that can span lines. Instead of concatenating lines with plus signs and escaping every quote and newline, you write the JSON or SQL as it looks. The compiler strips the incidental indentation that comes from your code layout, normalises line endings, and you can use a backslash at a line end to join lines or \\s to keep trailing spaces. They are great for SQL queries, JSON payloads in tests and HTML templates, and with the formatted method you can fill in values, like a format string.',
    'How is indentation inside a text block decided?', src),
  Q('nq-011', MJ, 'MS', 'concept', 'What is the pseudo-random generator API in Java?',
    [kp('Java 17 RandomGenerator interface unifies generators', 'randomgenerator', 'interface'), kp('RandomGeneratorFactory to choose an algorithm by name', 'randomgeneratorfactory', 'factory', 'algorithm'), kp('New algorithms such as L64X128MixRandom; splittable/jumpable', 'l64x128', 'splittable', 'jumpable', 'lxm'), kp('Not for security: use SecureRandom', 'securerandom', 'secur*')],
    'Java 17 (JEP 356) introduced a common RandomGenerator interface in java.util.random that Random, SplittableRandom, ThreadLocalRandom and SecureRandom now implement, so code can depend on the interface and swap algorithms. RandomGeneratorFactory lets you list and select algorithms by name, for example RandomGenerator.of("L64X128MixRandom"), a new family of fast, high quality LXM generators. There are sub interfaces for splittable, jumpable and leapable generators, useful for parallel streams and simulations where each thread needs an independent stream of numbers. These are pseudo random and not suitable for security; for tokens or keys use SecureRandom.',
    'Why should ThreadLocalRandom be preferred over a shared Random in multithreaded code?', src),
  Q('nq-012', MJ, 'MS', 'concept', 'What are sequenced collections in Java 21?',
    [kp('New interfaces SequencedCollection, SequencedSet, SequencedMap', 'sequencedcollection', 'sequencedset', 'sequencedmap'), kp('Uniform first/last access: getFirst, getLast, addFirst, removeLast', 'getfirst', 'getlast', 'addfirst', 'removelast'), kp('reversed() view', 'reversed'), kp('Implemented by List, Deque, LinkedHashSet, LinkedHashMap, TreeSet', 'list', 'deque', 'linkedhashset', 'linkedhashmap', 'treeset')],
    'Before Java 21, collections with a defined encounter order had no common API: getting the last element of a List was list.get(list.size() - 1), of a Deque getLast, and of a LinkedHashSet you had to iterate. Java 21 added SequencedCollection, SequencedSet and SequencedMap interfaces. They provide getFirst, getLast, addFirst, addLast, removeFirst, removeLast and reversed, which returns a reversed view, and maps get firstEntry, lastEntry, pollFirstEntry and sequencedKeySet. List, Deque, LinkedHashSet, LinkedHashMap, TreeSet and TreeMap implement them, so code can work with any ordered collection uniformly.',
    'Why does HashSet not implement SequencedSet?', src),
  Q('nq-013', MJ, 'SL', 'concept', 'What are scoped values in Java?',
    [kp('Immutable value shared within a bounded scope of a call', 'immutable', 'bounded', 'scope'), kp('Alternative to ThreadLocal, cheaper with virtual threads', 'threadlocal', 'virtual thread*'), kp('ScopedValue.where(KEY, value).run(...)', 'scopedvalue', 'where', 'run'), kp('Automatically inherited by structured concurrency child tasks; preview feature', 'structured concurrency', 'child', 'preview', 'inherit*')],
    'Scoped values, a preview in Java 21 and finalized in later releases, let you share an immutable value with all code called within a bounded scope, without passing it as a parameter. You declare static final ScopedValue<User> USER = ScopedValue.newInstance(), then ScopedValue.where(USER, currentUser).run(() -> handle()); anywhere inside handle you call USER.get(). Unlike ThreadLocal, the value cannot be changed, it is gone when the scope ends so nothing leaks, and it is cheap to inherit into child threads created by structured concurrency. That makes it a better fit than ThreadLocal for millions of virtual threads, for example for request context or security principal.',
    'What problems does ThreadLocal cause with virtual threads?', src),
  Q('nq-014', MJ, 'MS', 'concept', 'What are virtual threads, and how do they differ from platform threads?',
    [kp('Lightweight threads managed by the JVM, not 1:1 OS threads', 'lightweight', 'jvm', 'os thread*', 'managed by the jvm'), kp('Mounted on carrier platform threads; unmounted when blocking', 'carrier', 'mount*', 'unmount*', 'block*'), kp('Millions possible; cheap to create, no pooling needed', 'million*', 'cheap', 'pool*'), kp('Best for I/O-bound, thread-per-request code; not faster for CPU', 'i/o', 'io bound', 'thread per request', 'cpu')],
    'A platform thread is a thin wrapper around an operating system thread: it has a large stack, is expensive to create, and a server can only have a few thousand, so we pool them. A virtual thread, final in Java 21, is a lightweight thread managed by the JVM. Many virtual threads run on a small pool of carrier platform threads; when a virtual thread blocks on I/O, the JVM unmounts it and frees the carrier for another one, then remounts it when the I/O completes. So you can have millions of them and write simple blocking, thread per request code that scales like asynchronous code. They do not make CPU-bound work faster.',
    'How many carrier threads does the default scheduler use?', src),
  Q('nq-015', MJ, 'SL', 'concept', 'What are the advantages and limitations of virtual threads?',
    [kp('Advantages: high concurrency for blocking I/O, simple synchronous code, cheap', 'concurren*', 'simple', 'cheap', 'synchronous', 'blocking'), kp('Pinning with synchronized blocks or native calls (before Java 24)', 'pinn*', 'synchronized', 'native'), kp('No benefit for CPU-bound work', 'cpu'), kp('ThreadLocal heavy use costly; downstream limits still apply (connection pools)', 'threadlocal', 'connection pool*', 'downstream', 'semaphore', 'rate limit*'), kp('Do not pool virtual threads', 'pool*')],
    'Advantages: you can run a huge number of concurrent tasks with plain blocking code, no callbacks or reactive chains; creation is cheap so you start one per task instead of pooling; debugging and stack traces stay readable; and throughput of I/O bound services rises without tuning thread pools. Limitations: they do not speed up CPU bound work; until Java 24 a virtual thread that blocks inside a synchronized block or native call pins its carrier, so long synchronized sections should use ReentrantLock; heavy ThreadLocal caches multiply memory; and downstream resources such as database connection pools still limit concurrency, so you may need semaphores. Never pool virtual threads.',
    'How do you detect pinning?', src),
  Q('nq-016', MJ, 'SL', 'concept', 'How are virtual threads created, scheduled, and managed by the JVM and operating system?',
    [kp('Created with Thread.ofVirtual(), Thread.startVirtualThread, or an executor', 'thread.ofvirtual', 'startvirtualthread', 'newvirtualthreadpertaskexecutor'), kp('Scheduled by a ForkJoinPool of carrier threads (work stealing), not by the OS', 'forkjoinpool', 'carrier', 'work stealing', 'scheduler'), kp('Stack stored on the heap as continuation; mounted/unmounted', 'heap', 'continuation', 'mount*', 'unmount*'), kp('OS only sees the carrier platform threads', 'os', 'operating system', 'kernel')],
    'You create one with Thread.ofVirtual().start(task), Thread.startVirtualThread(task), or Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor(). The operating system knows nothing about them; it only schedules the carrier platform threads. The JVM has its own scheduler, a work stealing ForkJoinPool with by default as many carriers as CPU cores. A virtual thread is implemented as a continuation: when it runs, it is mounted on a carrier; when it blocks on a supported operation such as socket I/O, sleep or a lock, its stack frames are saved to the heap and it is unmounted, freeing the carrier. When the operation completes it is scheduled again, possibly on another carrier.',
    'What happens to a virtual thread blocked in a file read?', src),
  Q('nq-017', MJ, 'MS', 'concept', 'How do you use virtual threads with ExecutorService?',
    [kp('Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()', 'newvirtualthreadpertaskexecutor', 'per task'), kp('try-with-resources closes and waits for tasks', 'try with resources', 'try-with-resources', 'close', 'wait*'), kp('Submit tasks and collect Futures', 'submit', 'future*', 'invokeall'), kp('Spring Boot: spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true', 'spring.threads.virtual', 'spring boot')],
    'Use Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor(). It creates a new virtual thread for every submitted task instead of reusing a fixed pool. Typical code: try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) { List<Future<Price>> futures = suppliers.stream().map(s -> executor.submit(() -> fetchPrice(s))).toList(); ... } . ExecutorService is AutoCloseable since Java 19, so leaving the try block waits for all tasks to finish. You can also use invokeAll, or a thread factory Thread.ofVirtual().factory() with other executors. In Spring Boot 3.2+, spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true makes Tomcat and async tasks use virtual threads.',
    'Why should you not put virtual threads in a fixed-size pool?', src),
  Q('nq-018', MJ, 'SL', 'concept', 'How are resources released when a virtual thread completes execution?',
    [kp('Thread object and its heap-stored stack become garbage collected', 'garbage', 'gc', 'heap', 'collected'), kp('Carrier thread is returned to the scheduler', 'carrier', 'returned', 'scheduler'), kp('Application resources still need try-with-resources / finally', 'try with resources', 'try-with-resources', 'finally', 'close'), kp('ThreadLocals die with the thread', 'threadlocal*')],
    'When a virtual thread finishes, the JVM unmounts it from its carrier and the carrier immediately goes back to the scheduler to run other virtual threads; nothing is returned to the operating system because no OS thread was created. The virtual thread object and its stack chunks, which live on the heap, become unreachable and are reclaimed by the garbage collector, along with its ThreadLocal values. What the JVM does not release automatically are your own resources: open connections, files, locks or semaphore permits. Those must still be released with try-with-resources or finally blocks, and an ExecutorService used in try-with-resources waits for and closes its tasks.',
    'Why are virtual threads cheap to create and destroy?', src),

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2. Java 8 features, lambdas, functional interfaces
  Q('nq-019', J8, 'E', 'concept', 'What are the key features introduced in Java 8?',
    [kp('Lambda expressions and functional interfaces', 'lambda*', 'functional interface*'), kp('Stream API', 'stream*'), kp('Default and static methods in interfaces', 'default method*', 'static method*'), kp('Optional, method references', 'optional', 'method reference*'), kp('New Date/Time API, CompletableFuture', 'date', 'time api', 'java.time', 'completablefuture')],
    'Java 8, released in 2014, was the biggest change since generics. It added lambda expressions and functional interfaces, which bring functional programming style; method references like String::toUpperCase; the Stream API for declarative processing of collections, including parallel streams; default and static methods in interfaces, so interfaces can evolve; Optional to model a value that may be absent; the new java.time Date and Time API, immutable and thread safe; CompletableFuture for asynchronous composition; plus Nashorn, the Base64 class, and removal of PermGen in favour of Metaspace.',
    'Which Java 8 feature do you use most, and why?', src),
  Q('nq-020', J8, 'EM', 'concept', 'What is a lambda expression, and how does it work internally?',
    [kp('Anonymous function implementing a functional interface', 'anonymous function', 'functional interface'), kp('Syntax (params) -> body', 'arrow', 'syntax'), kp('Compiled with invokedynamic, no extra class file', 'invokedynamic', 'class file'), kp('LambdaMetafactory creates the implementation at runtime; private method in the class', 'lambdametafactory', 'private method', 'runtime')],
    'A lambda is a concise anonymous function, written as parameters, an arrow and a body, such as (a, b) -> a + b, that provides the implementation of the single abstract method of a functional interface, so behaviour can be passed as data. Internally the compiler does not generate an anonymous inner class file. It moves the lambda body into a private synthetic method in the enclosing class and emits an invokedynamic instruction at the creation site. The first time it runs, the bootstrap method LambdaMetafactory spins up an implementation of the interface that calls that method; for non-capturing lambdas the same instance is reused. This is lighter than inner classes and lets the JVM optimise.',
    'Why can a non-capturing lambda be a singleton?', src),
  Q('nq-021', J8, 'EM', 'concept', 'What is the difference between a lambda expression and an anonymous inner class?',
    [kp('this refers to enclosing instance in lambda, to the inner class instance in anonymous class', 'this'), kp('Lambda only for functional interfaces; anonymous class can implement any interface or extend a class', 'functional interface', 'abstract class', 'extend*', 'multiple methods'), kp('Anonymous class creates a .class file; lambda uses invokedynamic', 'class file', 'invokedynamic'), kp('Anonymous class can have state/fields; lambda cannot', 'state', 'field*'), kp('Shadowing: lambda cannot redeclare local variables', 'shadow*', 'scope')],
    'A lambda can only implement a functional interface, while an anonymous class can implement any interface with several methods or extend a class, even an abstract one, and can hold its own fields. Inside a lambda, this means the enclosing instance; inside an anonymous class, this is the anonymous object itself. A lambda shares the enclosing scope, so it cannot redeclare a local variable name, whereas an anonymous class introduces a new scope. The compiler generates a separate Outer$1.class for every anonymous class, while lambdas are linked at runtime with invokedynamic, giving less class loading and better performance. Lambdas are shorter and clearer for one-method behaviour.',
    'When would you still choose an anonymous class?', src),
  Q('nq-022', J8, 'EM', 'concept', 'What restrictions or constraints apply to lambda expressions?',
    [kp('Captured local variables must be final or effectively final', 'effectively final', 'final'), kp('Target type must be a functional interface', 'functional interface', 'target type'), kp('Checked exceptions not allowed unless declared by the interface', 'checked exception*'), kp('Cannot shadow local variables; this refers to enclosing class', 'shadow*', 'this', 'redeclare')],
    'A lambda needs a target type that is a functional interface, so it cannot stand alone, for example as Object o = () -> {} without a cast. Local variables it captures must be final or effectively final, meaning never reassigned; instance and static fields can be changed, which is a source of thread safety bugs. It cannot throw checked exceptions unless the functional interface method declares them, which is why using a method that throws IOException inside stream map needs wrapping. It cannot redeclare a variable already in the enclosing scope, and this refers to the enclosing object. It also cannot be generic itself.',
    'Why must captured variables be effectively final?', src),
  Q('nq-023', J8, 'E', 'concept', 'What is a functional interface?',
    [kp('Interface with exactly one abstract method (SAM)', 'one abstract method', 'single abstract method', 'sam'), kp('Can have default and static methods', 'default', 'static'), kp('Target for lambdas and method references', 'lambda*', 'method reference*'), kp('Examples Runnable, Comparator, Function', 'runnable', 'comparator', 'function', 'predicate')],
    'A functional interface is an interface with exactly one abstract method, also called a single abstract method or SAM type. It may still have any number of default and static methods, and methods that override Object methods such as equals do not count. Because there is only one method to implement, a lambda or method reference can supply it: Runnable r = () -> doWork(). Classic examples are Runnable, Callable and Comparator, and Java 8 added the java.util.function package with Function, Predicate, Supplier, Consumer and others. You can write your own and mark it with @FunctionalInterface.',
    'Is Comparator a functional interface even though it has many methods?', src),
  Q('nq-024', J8, 'E', 'concept', 'What is the purpose of the @FunctionalInterface annotation?',
    [kp('Compiler checks there is exactly one abstract method', 'compiler', 'compile', 'one abstract method', 'error'), kp('Documents intent for readers', 'document*', 'intent'), kp('Optional: interface is functional without it', 'optional', 'not required', 'without')],
    'It is an informative annotation that tells the compiler the interface is intended to be functional. If someone adds a second abstract method or removes the only one, compilation fails, which protects lambdas that already use it. It also documents the intent for other developers. It is optional: any interface with a single abstract method can be used with lambdas even without the annotation, but adding it on your own functional interfaces is good practice.',
    'Can a functional interface extend another interface?', { ...src, sameAs: 'jb-064' }),
  Q('nq-025', J8, 'E', 'concept', 'What are the commonly used built-in functional interfaces in Java?',
    [kp('Function<T,R> transforms', 'function'), kp('Predicate<T> tests a condition', 'predicate'), kp('Consumer<T> and Supplier<T>', 'consumer', 'supplier'), kp('BiFunction, UnaryOperator, BinaryOperator', 'bifunction', 'unaryoperator', 'binaryoperator'), kp('Primitive specialisations like IntPredicate, ToIntFunction', 'intpredicate', 'tointfunction', 'primitive')],
    'The java.util.function package has about forty, built around four shapes. Function<T, R> takes a T and returns an R, used in map. Predicate<T> returns a boolean, used in filter. Consumer<T> accepts a value and returns nothing, used in forEach. Supplier<T> takes nothing and returns a value, used for lazy creation such as orElseGet. Two-argument versions are BiFunction, BiPredicate and BiConsumer; UnaryOperator and BinaryOperator are functions whose input and output types are the same, used in replaceAll and reduce. Primitive versions such as IntPredicate, ToIntFunction and IntBinaryOperator avoid boxing. Older ones like Runnable, Callable and Comparator are also functional.',
    'Why do primitive specialisations exist?', src),
  Q('nq-026', J8, 'E', 'concept', 'Explain Predicate, Supplier, Consumer, and Function.',
    [kp('Predicate: T -> boolean, test()', 'predicate', 'boolean', 'test'), kp('Supplier: () -> T, get()', 'supplier', 'get'), kp('Consumer: T -> void, accept()', 'consumer', 'accept', 'void'), kp('Function: T -> R, apply(); composition andThen/compose', 'function', 'apply', 'andthen', 'compose')],
    'Predicate<T> represents a condition: its boolean test(T t) is used in filter, and predicates combine with and, or and negate. Supplier<T> produces a value without input through T get(), useful for lazy creation, factories, orElseGet and default values. Consumer<T> performs an action on a value through void accept(T t), as in forEach or logging, and andThen chains consumers. Function<T, R> transforms a value with R apply(T t), as in map, and functions compose with andThen and compose. For example: Predicate<String> notEmpty = s -> !s.isEmpty(); Function<String, Integer> length = String::length.',
    'How would you combine two predicates?', src),
  Q('nq-027', J8, 'E', 'concept', 'What is the purpose of the test() method in Predicate and the get() method in Supplier?',
    [kp('test(T) evaluates a condition and returns boolean', 'test', 'boolean', 'condition'), kp('Used by filter, removeIf, anyMatch', 'filter', 'removeif', 'anymatch'), kp('get() returns a value with no input, lazily', 'get', 'lazy', 'lazily', 'no input'), kp('Used by orElseGet, Stream.generate, ThreadLocal.withInitial', 'orelseget', 'generate', 'withinitial')],
    'test is the single abstract method of Predicate. It takes one argument and returns true or false, and every lambda you pass as a condition implements it; filter, removeIf, anyMatch and allMatch call test on each element. get is the single abstract method of Supplier. It takes no arguments and returns a value, so it represents a deferred or lazy computation: Optional.orElseGet(supplier) calls get only if the value is missing, Stream.generate calls it repeatedly, and ThreadLocal.withInitial uses it to create the initial value. Writing predicate.test(x) or supplier.get() is how you invoke them explicitly.',
    'Why is orElseGet with a Supplier more efficient than orElse?', src),
  Q('nq-028', J8, 'E', 'concept', 'What is the difference between Consumer and Function?',
    [kp('Consumer returns nothing (side effect)', 'consumer', 'void', 'nothing', 'side effect*'), kp('Function returns a result', 'function', 'return*', 'result'), kp('Consumer in forEach/peek, Function in map', 'foreach', 'peek', 'map'), kp('accept vs apply', 'accept', 'apply')],
    'Both take one input. A Consumer<T> has void accept(T t): it performs a side effect such as printing, saving or sending, and returns nothing, so it is used in forEach and peek. A Function<T, R> has R apply(T t): it transforms the input into a result, ideally without side effects, and is used in map. So if you want a new value, use Function; if you want to do something with the value, use Consumer. Both support andThen for chaining, and BiConsumer and BiFunction are the two argument versions.',
    'Why should functions passed to map avoid side effects?', src),
  Q('nq-029', J8, 'EM', 'concept', 'What are UnaryOperator and BinaryOperator?',
    [kp('UnaryOperator<T> extends Function<T,T>: same input/output type', 'unaryoperator', 'function<t,t>', 'same type'), kp('BinaryOperator<T> extends BiFunction<T,T,T>', 'binaryoperator', 'bifunction'), kp('Examples: replaceAll, reduce, identity, minBy/maxBy', 'replaceall', 'reduce', 'identity', 'minby', 'maxby')],
    'They are specialisations for operations whose inputs and output have the same type. UnaryOperator<T> extends Function<T, T>: one T in, one T out, such as String::trim or x -> x * 2; List.replaceAll takes a UnaryOperator, and UnaryOperator.identity() returns its input. BinaryOperator<T> extends BiFunction<T, T, T>: two T values in, one T out, such as Integer::sum, which is exactly what reduce needs, numbers.stream().reduce(0, Integer::sum). BinaryOperator also has static minBy and maxBy taking a Comparator. Primitive versions are IntUnaryOperator and IntBinaryOperator.',
    'Where else is BinaryOperator used in the Collections API?', src),
  Q('nq-030', J8, 'E', 'concept', 'What is the difference between unary and binary functional interfaces?',
    [kp('Unary takes one argument, binary takes two', 'one argument', 'two argument*', 'one input', 'two input*'), kp('Examples Function/UnaryOperator vs BiFunction/BinaryOperator', 'bifunction', 'binaryoperator', 'unaryoperator'), kp('Binary ones used for combining/reducing', 'reduce', 'combin*', 'merge')],
    'The difference is the number of arguments. Unary interfaces take one argument: Function, Predicate, Consumer and UnaryOperator. Binary interfaces take two: BiFunction, BiPredicate, BiConsumer and BinaryOperator. Binary forms are typically used to combine two values, such as reduce, Map.merge, or a Comparator, which compares two objects. In the Operator versions the arguments and result share one type, while the Function versions allow different types. If you need three arguments there is nothing built in, so you define your own functional interface.',
    'How would you model a function with three parameters?', src),
  Q('nq-031', J8, 'EM', 'concept', 'What are default and static methods in interfaces?',
    [kp('default method has a body and is inherited by implementations', 'default', 'body', 'implementation*'), kp('Allows adding methods without breaking implementations', 'backward compat*', 'without breaking', 'evolve'), kp('static methods are utility methods on the interface', 'static', 'utility', 'helper'), kp('Diamond conflict must be resolved by overriding', 'diamond', 'conflict', 'override', 'super')],
    'Since Java 8 interfaces can contain method bodies. A default method, declared with the default keyword, provides an implementation that every implementing class inherits but may override; List.sort and Collection.stream are examples. A static method belongs to the interface itself and is called through the interface name, used for helpers and factories such as Comparator.comparing or List.of. Default methods allow interfaces to evolve without breaking existing implementations. If a class inherits the same default method from two interfaces, it must override it and can call a specific one with InterfaceName.super.method(). Java 9 also allowed private interface methods.',
    'How do you resolve the diamond problem with two default methods?', src),
  Q('nq-032', J8, 'M', 'concept', 'How do default methods support backward compatibility?',
    [kp('Adding an abstract method would break all existing implementations', 'break', 'compile', 'existing implementation*'), kp('A default method supplies an implementation so old classes still compile', 'default', 'still compile', 'implementation'), kp('Example: Collection.stream(), forEach, List.sort added in Java 8', 'stream()', 'foreach', 'list.sort'), kp('Library evolution', 'evolv*', 'library')],
    'If you add a new abstract method to a published interface, every class that implements it, including classes in other teams or third party libraries, stops compiling until it implements the method. Default methods solve that: the new method comes with an implementation, so existing implementations keep compiling and working and simply inherit it, while new ones can override it with something better. That is how Java 8 added stream, forEach, removeIf and spliterator to Collection and Iterable, and sort to List, without breaking millions of existing collection classes. You can use the same technique when evolving your own APIs.',
    'Can default methods break anything?', src),
  Q('nq-033', J8, 'E', 'concept', 'How do you call a static interface method?',
    [kp('Through the interface name: Interface.method()', 'interface name', 'interfacename.', 'comparator.comparing'), kp('Not inherited by implementing classes or instances', 'not inherited', 'instance', 'implementing class'), kp('Cannot be overridden', 'overrid*')],
    'You call it with the interface name, such as Comparator.naturalOrder(), List.of(1, 2) or MyValidator.isValid(x). Unlike static methods in classes, static interface methods are not inherited: you cannot call them through an implementing class name or through an instance, and the implementing class cannot override them, it can only declare its own method with the same signature. This keeps them as utilities attached to the interface, for example factory methods that return implementations.',
    'Why are static interface methods not inherited?', src),
  Q('nq-034', J8, 'E', 'concept', 'What improvements were introduced in Java 8 for date and time handling?',
    [kp('java.time package (JSR-310) replacing Date/Calendar', 'java.time', 'jsr', 'date', 'calendar'), kp('Immutable and thread-safe', 'immutable', 'thread safe', 'thread-safe'), kp('LocalDate, LocalDateTime, ZonedDateTime, Instant', 'localdate', 'localdatetime', 'zoneddatetime', 'instant'), kp('Duration/Period and DateTimeFormatter', 'duration', 'period', 'datetimeformatter')],
    'Java 8 introduced the java.time package, based on Joda-Time. The old java.util.Date and Calendar were mutable, not thread safe, had months starting at zero and mixed date and time zone concerns, and SimpleDateFormat was not thread safe. The new classes are immutable and thread safe, with clear types: LocalDate for a date, LocalTime, LocalDateTime without a zone, ZonedDateTime and OffsetDateTime with a zone or offset, and Instant for a machine timestamp. Duration and Period represent amounts of time, DateTimeFormatter is thread safe, and fluent methods such as plusDays and withDayOfMonth return new objects.',
    'Which type would you store in the database for an event timestamp?', src),
  Q('nq-035', J8, 'EM', 'concept', 'What are method references, and what are their different types?',
    [kp('Shorthand for a lambda that only calls an existing method, ::', 'shorthand', 'double colon', 'existing method'), kp('Static method reference Class::staticMethod', 'static', 'integer::parseint', 'math::'), kp('Instance method of a particular object obj::method', 'particular object', 'system.out::println', 'bound'), kp('Instance method of an arbitrary object Class::instanceMethod', 'arbitrary', 'string::touppercase', 'unbound'), kp('Constructor reference Class::new', 'constructor', '::new')],
    'A method reference is a shorter form of a lambda whose body just calls an existing method, written with the double colon. There are four kinds. A static method reference, Integer::parseInt, equals s -> Integer.parseInt(s). A reference to an instance method of a particular object, System.out::println or this::validate, calls the method on that object. A reference to an instance method of an arbitrary object of a type, String::toUpperCase, uses the first argument as the receiver, so it equals s -> s.toUpperCase(). A constructor reference, ArrayList::new or Product::new, creates objects. They improve readability when the method name says it all.',
    'What does String::compareToIgnoreCase map to as a Comparator?', src),

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3. Stream API and parallel streams
  Q('nq-036', J8, 'E', 'concept', 'What is the Stream API, and what problems does it solve?',
    [kp('Declarative processing of sequences of elements', 'declarative', 'sequence'), kp('Replaces verbose loops; readable pipelines of filter/map/collect', 'loop*', 'pipeline', 'filter', 'map', 'collect'), kp('Does not store data or modify the source', 'does not store', 'source', 'not modify'), kp('Lazy evaluation and easy parallelism', 'lazy', 'parallel')],
    'A stream is a sequence of elements from a source such as a collection, array, file or generator, supporting aggregate operations. Instead of writing how to loop, with index variables and temporary lists, you declare what you want: orders.stream().filter(o -> o.isPaid()).map(Order::total).reduce(0, Integer::sum). That makes code shorter, more readable and less error prone. A stream does not store data and does not modify its source, it can be consumed only once, its intermediate operations are lazy so work is fused into one pass and can short circuit, and switching to parallel processing is a one-word change.',
    'Can a stream be reused after a terminal operation?', src),
  Q('nq-037', J8, 'E', 'concept', 'What are the stages of a stream pipeline?',
    [kp('Source: collection.stream(), Arrays.stream, Stream.of', 'source', 'stream()', 'stream.of', 'arrays.stream'), kp('Zero or more intermediate operations', 'intermediate'), kp('One terminal operation that produces a result', 'terminal'), kp('Nothing runs until the terminal operation', 'lazy', 'until')],
    'A stream pipeline has three parts. First a source that creates the stream: list.stream(), Arrays.stream(array), Stream.of(...), Files.lines(path) or IntStream.range. Second, zero or more intermediate operations such as filter, map, flatMap, sorted, distinct and limit, each returning a new stream and being lazy. Third, exactly one terminal operation, such as collect, forEach, reduce, count, findFirst or anyMatch, which triggers processing and produces a result or side effect. After the terminal operation the stream is consumed and cannot be reused.',
    'What happens if you build a pipeline without a terminal operation?', src),
  Q('nq-038', J8, 'E', 'concept', 'What are intermediate and terminal operations? Give examples.',
    [kp('Intermediate return a Stream and are lazy', 'intermediate', 'return* a stream', 'lazy'), kp('Examples filter, map, flatMap, sorted, distinct, limit, peek', 'filter', 'map', 'flatmap', 'sorted', 'distinct'), kp('Terminal produce a result and close the stream', 'terminal', 'result'), kp('Examples collect, forEach, reduce, count, findFirst, anyMatch', 'collect', 'foreach', 'reduce', 'count', 'findfirst'), kp('Stateless vs stateful; short-circuiting', 'stateful', 'stateless', 'short circuit*')],
    'Intermediate operations transform a stream into another stream and are lazy: filter, map, flatMap, peek, distinct, sorted, limit and skip. Some are stateless, like filter and map, while sorted and distinct are stateful because they need to see many elements; limit is short circuiting. Terminal operations start the processing and produce a non-stream result or a side effect: collect, toList, forEach, reduce, count, min, max, findFirst, findAny, anyMatch, allMatch and noneMatch. The last few are short circuiting, so they can stop early. A pipeline has any number of intermediate operations and exactly one terminal one.',
    'Why is sorted() called a stateful operation?', src),
  Q('nq-039', J8, 'EM', 'concept', 'What is lazy evaluation, and when does a stream pipeline execute?',
    [kp('Intermediate operations only build the pipeline', 'build*', 'pipeline', 'nothing happens', 'not executed'), kp('Execution starts at the terminal operation', 'terminal'), kp('Elements flow one at a time through all stages (loop fusion)', 'one at a time', 'each element', 'single pass', 'fusion'), kp('Short-circuiting saves work, e.g. findFirst, limit', 'short circuit*', 'findfirst', 'limit', 'infinite')],
    'Calling filter or map does not process anything; it only records the operation in the pipeline. Execution happens when a terminal operation is invoked. Then elements are pulled through all stages one at a time, vertically: the first element goes through filter, then map, then into the collector, before the second element is read. That fuses the stages into a single pass, and allows short circuiting: with filter followed by findFirst, processing stops as soon as one match is found, which is why you can even use infinite streams like Stream.iterate with limit. You can see this by adding peek with prints, which print nothing until the terminal operation.',
    'How does laziness let you work with infinite streams?', src),
  Q('nq-040', J8, 'E', 'concept', 'What is the difference between map(), filter(), sorted(), collect(), and forEach()?',
    [kp('filter keeps elements matching a predicate', 'filter', 'predicate', 'keep*'), kp('map transforms each element', 'map', 'transform*'), kp('sorted orders elements (natural or comparator)', 'sorted', 'order*', 'comparator'), kp('collect gathers results into a collection/map (terminal)', 'collect', 'list', 'collectors'), kp('forEach performs an action, returns void (terminal)', 'foreach', 'void', 'action')],
    'filter, map and sorted are intermediate operations returning a stream. filter takes a Predicate and keeps only matching elements, so the count can shrink. map takes a Function and transforms each element into another value, keeping the count. sorted orders elements by natural order or by a Comparator you pass, and is stateful. collect and forEach are terminal operations. collect uses a Collector, such as Collectors.toList, toSet, toMap, joining or groupingBy, to build a result container. forEach runs a Consumer for each element and returns nothing, so it is for side effects like printing, not for building collections.',
    'Why is collecting with forEach into an external list discouraged?', src),
  Q('nq-041', J8, 'E', 'concept', 'What does map() return, and what does forEach() return?',
    [kp('map returns a new Stream of transformed elements', 'map', 'stream', 'new stream'), kp('forEach returns void', 'foreach', 'void', 'nothing'), kp('map is intermediate/lazy, forEach is terminal', 'intermediate', 'terminal', 'lazy')],
    'map is an intermediate operation that returns a new Stream<R> containing the results of applying the function to each element, for example Stream<String> to Stream<Integer> with String::length. Because it returns a stream you can keep chaining, and it is lazy, so nothing happens until a terminal operation. forEach is a terminal operation that returns void: it runs the action for each element and ends the pipeline. So map is for producing transformed values, and forEach is for side effects at the end.',
    'Can you chain another operation after forEach?', src),
  Q('nq-042', J8, 'EM', 'concept', 'Does map() modify existing objects or create transformed values?',
    [kp('map produces new values; the source collection is not modified', 'new value*', 'source', 'not modif*', 'does not modif*'), kp('But a mapper that calls setters mutates the shared objects (side effect)', 'setter*', 'mutat*', 'side effect*', 'same object'), kp('Prefer creating new objects or records for immutability', 'new object*', 'immutable', 'record*')],
    'map is designed to produce new values: each element is passed to the function and whatever it returns flows downstream, while the source collection itself is never changed. Whether the original objects change depends on your function. If it builds a new object, p -> new Product(p.name(), p.price() * 0.85), the originals are untouched. If it calls a setter and returns the same reference, p -> { p.setPrice(...); return p; }, it mutates the shared objects, which is a side effect that surprises callers and is unsafe in parallel streams. Good practice is to keep map functions pure and use immutable objects or records.',
    'Why are side effects in map especially dangerous with parallel streams?', src),
  Q('nq-043', J8, 'EM', 'concept', 'What is the difference between map() and mapToDouble()?',
    [kp('map returns Stream<R> of objects (boxed)', 'stream<', 'object*', 'box*'), kp('mapToDouble returns a DoubleStream of primitives', 'doublestream', 'primitive*'), kp('Avoids boxing; gives sum(), average(), summaryStatistics()', 'boxing', 'sum', 'average', 'summarystatistics'), kp('mapToObj/boxed to go back', 'boxed', 'mapToObj')],
    'map returns a Stream<R> of objects, so mapping prices with map(Product::getPrice) gives a Stream<Double> of boxed Double objects. mapToDouble takes a ToDoubleFunction and returns a DoubleStream of primitive doubles, which avoids boxing and unboxing overhead and gives numeric terminal operations directly: sum, average, min, max and summaryStatistics. So products.stream().mapToDouble(Product::getPrice).sum() is the idiomatic way to total prices. mapToInt and mapToLong are the int and long versions, and from a primitive stream you return to objects with boxed or mapToObj.',
    'Why does average() return an OptionalDouble?', src),
  Q('nq-044', J8, 'E', 'concept', 'What are sequential and parallel streams?',
    [kp('Sequential processes elements one by one in a single thread', 'sequential', 'single thread', 'one by one'), kp('Parallel splits the source and processes chunks on multiple threads', 'parallel', 'multiple thread*', 'split*', 'chunk*'), kp('Created with parallelStream() or .parallel()', 'parallelstream', '.parallel'), kp('Uses the common ForkJoinPool', 'forkjoin', 'common pool', 'forkjoinpool')],
    'By default a stream is sequential: elements are processed one after another in the calling thread, in encounter order. A parallel stream, created with collection.parallelStream() or stream.parallel(), splits the source into chunks using a Spliterator, processes the chunks concurrently on worker threads of the common ForkJoinPool plus the calling thread, and then combines the partial results. The API and results are the same if your operations are stateless, non-interfering and associative, but order of side effects such as forEach is not guaranteed. You can switch back with sequential(); the last call wins for the whole pipeline.',
    'How many threads does a parallel stream use by default?', src),
  Q('nq-045', J8, 'MS', 'concept', 'How do parallel streams work internally?',
    [kp('Spliterator recursively splits the source', 'spliterator', 'split*'), kp('Fork/Join tasks on the common ForkJoinPool with work stealing', 'fork/join', 'forkjoinpool', 'work stealing'), kp('Each chunk processed independently, results combined (combiner)', 'combin*', 'merge', 'partial result*'), kp('Pool size = cores - 1, plus calling thread', 'cores', 'availableprocessors', 'calling thread')],
    'A parallel stream asks the source Spliterator to split itself; trySplit is called recursively until chunks are small enough. Each chunk becomes a ForkJoinTask run in the common ForkJoinPool, whose parallelism is by default the number of cores minus one, and the calling thread also helps. Each task applies the whole pipeline to its chunk, producing a partial result: a partial list, sum or map. Then results are combined up the tree, using the reduce combiner or the collector combiner. Idle workers steal tasks from busy workers queues to balance load. How well it works depends on how cheaply the source splits: ArrayList and arrays split well, LinkedList and iterate do not.',
    'Why does a parallel stream over a LinkedList perform badly?', src),
  Q('nq-046', J8, 'MS', 'concept', 'What is the role of the Fork/Join framework and ForkJoinPool in parallel streams?',
    [kp('Divide and conquer: fork subtasks, join results', 'divide and conquer', 'fork', 'join'), kp('ForkJoinPool with work-stealing deques', 'work stealing', 'deque*', 'forkjoinpool'), kp('Parallel streams use ForkJoinPool.commonPool()', 'commonpool', 'common pool'), kp('Blocking tasks starve the shared pool; can run in a custom pool', 'block*', 'starv*', 'custom pool', 'shared')],
    'The Fork/Join framework, from Java 7, implements divide and conquer: a RecursiveTask splits work into subtasks with fork, computes them in parallel and combines results with join. ForkJoinPool runs these tasks; each worker has its own deque and idle workers steal work from the tail of others deques, keeping all cores busy with little contention. Parallel streams are built on it: their split pieces become fork join tasks executed in ForkJoinPool.commonPool, shared by the whole JVM, including CompletableFuture async methods by default. So a parallel stream doing blocking I/O can starve every other user of the common pool; such work should go to a dedicated executor.',
    'How can you run a parallel stream in a custom ForkJoinPool?', src),
  Q('nq-047', J8, 'MS', 'concept', 'What are the advantages, disadvantages, and limitations of parallel streams?',
    [kp('Advantage: easy use of multiple cores for CPU-bound, large data', 'multiple core*', 'cpu', 'large'), kp('Overhead of splitting, threads and merging; slower for small data', 'overhead', 'small', 'slower'), kp('Shared common pool, blocking I/O starves it', 'common pool', 'block*', 'starv*'), kp('Ordering and side-effect issues; need stateless, associative operations', 'order*', 'side effect*', 'associative', 'stateless'), kp('Poorly splittable sources (LinkedList, iterate)', 'linkedlist', 'split*')],
    'The advantage is that you can use all cores for CPU intensive work on large data sets with almost no code change. The disadvantages: splitting, scheduling and merging have overhead, so on small collections or cheap operations a parallel stream is usually slower; all parallel streams share the common ForkJoinPool, so one slow or blocking stream affects others; encounter order and ordered operations like findFirst, limit and sorted become expensive; side effects on shared mutable state cause race conditions; and sources such as LinkedList, Stream.iterate or I/O streams split badly. Always measure with a benchmark before using one.',
    'Can you give a case where a parallel stream gives a wrong result?', src),
  Q('nq-048', J8, 'MS', 'scenario', 'How can shared mutable state cause problems in parallel streams?',
    [kp('Multiple threads update the same object without synchronisation', 'multiple thread*', 'same', 'shared', 'synchroni*'), kp('Race conditions: lost updates, wrong counts', 'race condition*', 'lost update*', 'wrong', 'race', 'lost'), kp('Non-thread-safe collections like ArrayList can corrupt or throw', 'arraylist', 'corrupt*', 'arrayindexoutofbounds', 'exception'), kp('Use collect/reduce instead of mutating external state', 'collect', 'reduce')],
    'In a parallel stream several threads run your lambdas at the same time. If they write to the same external variable or collection, for example list::add on an ArrayList declared outside, or counter[0]++, the updates race: some are lost, the list can end up with nulls or fewer elements, or throw ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException during an internal resize, and results change from run to run. The code may look fine in tests and fail under load. The fix is not to share state: let the stream produce the result with collect, toList, reduce, sum or groupingBy, which give each thread its own container and merge them safely.',
    'Is using Collections.synchronizedList a good fix?', src),
  Q('nq-049', J8, 'MS', 'concept', 'How can you ensure thread safety when using parallel streams?',
    [kp('Keep lambdas stateless and side-effect free', 'stateless', 'side effect*', 'pure'), kp('Use collect/reduce/Collectors which merge per-thread containers', 'collect', 'reduce', 'collectors'), kp('Concurrent collectors / ConcurrentHashMap, groupingByConcurrent', 'concurrent', 'groupingbyconcurrent', 'concurrenthashmap'), kp('Atomic variables if you must share counters; associative operations', 'atomic*', 'longadder', 'associative'), kp('Do not mutate source during processing (non-interference)', 'interfer*', 'source')],
    'Write the pipeline so it does not need synchronisation. Lambdas should be stateless and pure, not reading or writing shared mutable variables, and they must not modify the source while it is being processed. Build results with terminal operations designed for parallelism: collect with Collectors, toList, reduce with an identity and an associative accumulator and combiner, or sum. Collectors.groupingByConcurrent and toConcurrentMap use a ConcurrentHashMap when order does not matter. If you truly need a shared counter use LongAdder or AtomicLong, but that adds contention. Immutable domain objects make all of this much easier.',
    'Why must the reduce operation be associative?', src),
  Q('nq-050', J8, 'SL', 'scenario', 'When should you use a parallel stream, and what factors should you consider?',
    [kp('Large data set and CPU-intensive per-element work (N x Q model)', 'large', 'cpu', 'n*q', 'per element', 'expensive'), kp('Source splits well: ArrayList, arrays, ranges', 'arraylist', 'array*', 'range', 'split*'), kp('Independent, stateless operations; no ordering need', 'independent', 'stateless', 'order*'), kp('Not for blocking I/O or in web servers sharing the common pool', 'i/o', 'block*', 'server', 'common pool'), kp('Measure with benchmarks (JMH)', 'measure', 'benchmark', 'jmh')],
    'Consider a parallel stream only when the total work is large: many elements times a costly operation per element, the so-called N times Q rule, typically tens of thousands of elements doing real CPU work such as parsing, image or math processing. The source must split cheaply, like ArrayList, arrays or IntStream.range; operations must be independent, stateless and associative; you should not need ordered results or ordered side effects; and the machine must have free cores. Avoid them for blocking I/O, inside web request handlers where the common pool is shared, and for small lists. Always measure with JMH or realistic timing before and after.',
    'Why might a parallel stream hurt a Spring Boot service under load?', src),
  Q('nq-051', J8, 'E', 'concept', 'What is the Optional class, and how does it handle potentially absent values?',
    [kp('Container that may or may not hold a non-null value', 'container', 'may or may not', 'absent', 'empty'), kp('Avoids NullPointerException; makes absence explicit in the API', 'nullpointerexception', 'npe', 'explicit'), kp('Creation: of, ofNullable, empty', 'ofnullable', 'optional.of', 'optional.empty'), kp('Use map, filter, orElse, ifPresent instead of get()', 'map', 'orelse', 'ifpresent', 'get()'), kp('Use as return type, not for fields/parameters', 'return type', 'field*', 'parameter*')],
    'Optional<T>, from Java 8, is a container that either holds a non-null value or is empty. Returning Optional from a method, like findById, tells callers that a result may be missing and forces them to handle it, instead of returning null and risking a NullPointerException. Create it with Optional.of(value), Optional.ofNullable(maybeNull) or Optional.empty(). Work with it functionally: map and flatMap to transform, filter, ifPresent and ifPresentOrElse to act, and orElse, orElseGet or orElseThrow to get a value. Avoid calling get without checking. Use it mainly as a return type, not for fields, parameters or collections.',
    'Why is Optional not Serializable, and what does that tell you?', src),
  Q('nq-052', J8, 'EM', 'concept', 'What is the difference between orElse(), orElseGet(), and orElseThrow()?',
    [kp('orElse(value) always evaluates the default argument', 'orelse', 'always', 'evaluat*', 'eager*'), kp('orElseGet(supplier) evaluates lazily only if empty', 'orelseget', 'supplier', 'lazy', 'lazily', 'only if'), kp('orElseThrow throws NoSuchElementException or a supplied exception', 'orelsethrow', 'nosuchelementexception', 'exception'), kp('Use orElseGet for expensive defaults', 'expensive', 'database', 'call')],
    'All three return the contained value when present and differ when empty. orElse(other) returns the given default, but the argument is evaluated before the call, even when the Optional has a value, so orElse(createDefaultUser()) or orElse(repository.load()) always runs that code. orElseGet(supplier) takes a Supplier and calls it only when the Optional is empty, so it is the right choice for expensive or side-effecting defaults. orElseThrow() without arguments throws NoSuchElementException, and orElseThrow(() -> new UserNotFoundException(id)) throws your exception, which is typical in service layers mapped to a 404.',
    'Can you show a bug caused by using orElse with a method call?', src),
];
